#reference 1
@Author Cornell et al.
@Date 1995
W. D. Cornell, P. Cieplak, C. I. Bayly, I. R. Gould, K. M. Merz Jr., D. M. Ferguson, D. C. Spellmeyer, T. Fox, J. W. Caldwell and P. A. Kollman, "A Second Generation Force Field for the Simulation of Proteins, Nucleic Acids, and Organic Molecules", J. Am. Chem. Soc. 117, 5179-5197 (1995), https://doi.org/10.1021/ja00124a002. Table 14 (bond, angle, torsion, improper and van der Waals parameters; the torsion barriers are divided by the number of paths of the parm94 file so that every row is per dihedral) and Figures 4-6 (RESP charges of the residues).

#reference 2
@Author AmberTools
@Date 2023
Converted from the AmberTools 23 distribution (https://ambermd.org): parm/parm94.dat ("PARM94 for DNA, RNA and proteins with TIP3P Water") and the ff94 residue libraries lib/all_amino94.lib, all_aminont94.lib, all_aminoct94.lib, all_nucleic94.lib, ions94.lib and the TIP3P unit of lib/solvents.lib. The Amber force-field parameter files are in the public domain. The per-type SMARTS templates for molecules outside the residue libraries were written for xnn after the atom-type descriptions of Table 1 of reference 1; the residue SMARTS are generated from the library connectivity with element, degree and hydrogen-count constraints and atom maps in library order.

#reference 3
@Author Jorgensen et al.
@Date 1983
W. L. Jorgensen, J. Chandrasekhar, J. D. Madura, R. W. Impey and M. L. Klein, "Comparison of simple potential functions for simulating liquid water", J. Chem. Phys. 79, 926-935 (1983), https://doi.org/10.1063/1.445869 (TIP3P).

#reference 4
@Author Aqvist; Smith and Dang
@Date 1990; 1994
J. Aqvist, "Ion-water interaction potentials derived from free energy perturbation simulations", J. Phys. Chem. 94, 8021-8024 (1990), https://doi.org/10.1021/j100384a009 (alkali cations, adapted); D. E. Smith and L. X. Dang, "Computer simulations of NaCl association in polarizable water", J. Chem. Phys. 100, 3757-3766 (1994), https://doi.org/10.1063/1.466363 (chloride).

#end
